{:edb/db-before-t 2543 :edb/db-after-t 2544 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535856 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:05:53.807Z" 13194139535856 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370570 13194139535856 true] [17592186221342 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370577 13194139535856 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370584 13194139535856 true] [17592186221343 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370591 13194139535856 true] [17592186221344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370598 13194139535856 true] [17592186370570 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370571 13194139535856 true] [17592186370570 1089 "q-334645" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370570 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535856 true] [17592186370570 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.16,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.16)=0.40.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.04,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.04)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139535856 true] [17592186370570 1113 #uuid "792e1f89-f2c3-5839-895c-6eb10fc13178" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370570 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.16$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.16}{0.04}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.40$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.40}{0.005}=80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.161… \\\\ p & ≈2.161….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370572 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370573 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370574 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370575 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370576 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1041 "selection" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370573 13194139535856 true] [17592186370571 1110 #uuid "e1eb6629-9439-5577-b4ff-137e6f6c8e2b" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370572 1034 "3" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370572 1111 #uuid "a0487951-0215-5ba5-aaae-47f271b10528" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370573 1034 "2" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370573 1111 #uuid "304e1c61-b18a-5cc8-bbde-10cb0e711781" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370574 1034 "1" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370574 1111 #uuid "173db8f7-a715-5a70-a359-ff61ba75d31a" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370575 1034 "4" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370575 1111 #uuid "32c0d0ef-8149-5f2a-bb97-be3f1037476c" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370576 1034 "5" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370576 1111 #uuid "7f9bfed9-6016-5ea2-bff0-f2d0a363d5db" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370577 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370578 13194139535856 true] [17592186370577 1089 "q-334647" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535856 true] [17592186370577 1100 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.15,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.15)=0.54.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.003.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139535856 true] [17592186370577 1113 #uuid "a2ce911a-8124-5a66-812b-ed79e2f51c04" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370577 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.05}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.54$ to $0.003.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.54}{0.003}=180.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈180 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(180) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.726… \\\\ p & ≈3.726….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370579 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370580 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370581 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370582 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370583 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1041 "selection" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370582 13194139535856 true] [17592186370578 1110 #uuid "7eebf7f8-1795-5cfe-ae23-6bcc181442dc" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370579 1034 "3" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370579 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370579 1111 #uuid "8953ec9f-5675-5bc4-95c3-3798aef841f3" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370580 1034 "2" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370580 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370580 1111 #uuid "131c99ee-42c2-5fd2-a654-1742675953d6" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370581 1034 "1" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370581 1111 #uuid "54489bf9-5fc9-58a6-80ba-096dff2ab70a" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370582 1034 "4" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370582 1111 #uuid "6e6aba09-ca9c-54bf-970a-5772b1b3dcbd" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370583 1034 "5" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370583 1111 #uuid "ebde9b7c-9901-5cdf-9732-773857fc0167" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370584 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370585 13194139535856 true] [17592186370584 1089 "q-334753" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370584 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535856 true] [17592186370584 1100 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}$" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370584 1113 #uuid "f0bdeb7f-c72e-54d6-b6ac-05f132bb2bb4" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370584 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{3}=0.3(Δx)^{2+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{5}=9(Δx)^{4+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370586 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370587 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370588 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370589 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370590 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1041 "selection" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370590 13194139535856 true] [17592186370585 1110 #uuid "92178e62-4839-510e-bdbb-8210fc101bc4" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370586 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370586 1111 #uuid "c2eafb80-10b8-5c87-8b44-1dc304f7126c" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370587 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370587 1111 #uuid "9f4f43f4-400d-5ca2-8008-3681f9b38f15" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370588 1034 "I only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370588 1111 #uuid "fba7862b-16e7-5d45-ace7-46229633ea7f" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370589 1034 "III only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370589 1111 #uuid "3d538766-072c-5796-ac87-dab54b04e164" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370590 1034 "II only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370590 1111 #uuid "52ca5300-a24a-51d9-ae3d-fba9fbf488bf" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370591 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370592 13194139535856 true] [17592186370591 1089 "q-334756" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370591 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535856 true] [17592186370591 1100 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3}$" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370591 1113 #uuid "c07fc2d9-cd40-5751-8c9f-7d5d62132eff" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370591 1145 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370593 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370594 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370595 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370596 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370597 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1041 "selection" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370596 13194139535856 true] [17592186370592 1110 #uuid "22df5fc2-30a5-5903-88d4-35266894a4aa" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370593 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370593 1111 #uuid "b4930550-2c52-5b12-9884-97e54135e4ce" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370594 1034 "I only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370594 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370594 1111 #uuid "1c5dc261-951f-58e4-ac81-9cfc8e8e0563" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370595 1034 "III only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370595 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370595 1111 #uuid "77bf41e8-3f2f-5c35-85fb-e8c0ff8298b9" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370596 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370596 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370596 1111 #uuid "4b1dce58-ba5e-5ecd-9da7-1f506c957a29" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370597 1034 "II only" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535856 true] [17592186370597 1111 #uuid "a2ccfa72-c444-53c8-8489-a6f5ff529e4c" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370598 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370599 13194139535856 true] [17592186370598 1089 "q-334951" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370598 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535856 true] [17592186370598 1100 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.24$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.24)≈0.32.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.12,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.12)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370598 1113 #uuid "7cc8c4d7-f786-5b1c-a1c0-7482e91fbcb2" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370598 1145 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.24$ to $0.12.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.24}{0.12}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={2}^{4}=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.12,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.12)≈\\frac{0.32}{16}=0.02.\n$$" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370599 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370600 13194139535856 true] [17592186370599 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535856 true] [17592186370599 1041 "field-1" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370599 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370600 13194139535856 true] [17592186370599 1110 #uuid "54f51b0b-b0a6-5a71-8620-163ab26d94f5" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370600 1034 "0.02" 13194139535856 true] [17592186370600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535856 true] [17592186370600 1111 #uuid "6ad43846-bee9-5c21-b5fe-83afbabe445d" 13194139535856 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-334645" 17592186370570 "q-334645/selection" 17592186370571 "q-334645/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" 17592186370572 "q-334645/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" 17592186370573 "q-334645/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" 17592186370574 "q-334645/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" 17592186370575 "q-334645/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" 17592